From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Smith <scott@newtrostudios.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 20:36:22 -0400
Subject: remotesmith(0701): direct port accepts LAN sources on LAN interfaces
 only

DEF-14, AC-51 (whole-product review finding 1). Upstream's direct server listens on every
interface and serves any source, and the Windows installer allows every inbound packet to the
program. Now:

- macOS (weak host model): the direct server no longer listens on the wildcard address but on
  each address of each LAN interface, each socket pinned to its interface (IP_BOUND_IF /
  IPV6_BOUND_IF), refreshed every 3 s, so a handshake that arrived over utun or loopback never
  completes even when the VPN routes an overlapping subnet. Windows uses the strong host model,
  so the adapter owning the local address is the one that received the connection.
- Windows adapters must be hardware-backed (GetIfTable2 HardwareInterface); a virtual adapter
  counts only if it carries a hardware adapter's MAC (a Hyper-V external switch's host adapter).
  The hardware-MAC inventory comes from the full GetIfTable2 table, since the IP enumeration
  omits a NIC an external switch unbound from IP.
- direct_server checks each accepted connection before any handshake byte: the local address
  it reached must belong to a physical LAN interface (macOS enN; Windows wired/Wi-Fi adapters
  that are not a VPN product's virtual adapter; never loopback, utun/tun/tap, ppp, ipsec,
  bridge, WireGuard, Tailscale, ZeroTier, ...), and the source must be on that address's
  subnet. Anything else is dropped unread and logged (rate-limited). Unknown interfaces fail
  closed. The interface list is cached for 2 s, so a flood of the port is not a flood of
  enumerations. Limitation: a VPN gateway or router on the LAN that NATs remote users onto its
  own LAN address looks like a LAN host; that is outside what a layer-3 check can see.
- Windows (netsh install script and the MSI custom action): the inbound program rule is split
  into UDP, TCP on every port but 21118 (as upstream), and TCP 21118 from LocalSubnet only,
  on LAN and wireless interface types only, without edge traversal. Same rule name, so uninstall still removes them all; the MSI remove
  now loops until none is left.

Known limits (deferred; need an attacker on a VPN with an overlapping LAN prefix; identity and
password layers still apply): IP_BOUND_IF pins the reply path but is no ingress filter against
asymmetric-routing/proxy-ARP VPN setups; Windows VPN clients that enable weak host mode (e.g.
GlobalProtect) defeat the destination-owner check.

